//     restated it is.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read out of AP_CS_P_Starter_Project, Unity 6000.6.0f1, by
// parsing Assets/_Scenes/Platformer.unity and the asset folders directly):
//
//   Platformer ▸ Grid — m_CellSize: {x: 1, y: 1, z: 0}. It has THREE tilemap
//     children, all with m_Father pointing at the Grid's Transform:
//       `Collision`    75 tiles, bounding box x -8..7 (16 wide) and y -5..4
//                      (10 tall) — a cave shape, NOT a filled rectangle.
//                      Tag `Ground`, layer 0. TilemapCollider2D
//                      (m_CompositeOperation: 1 = Merge), CompositeCollider2D,
//                      Rigidbody2D m_BodyType: 2 (Static), m_GravityScale: 1.
//       `Cave Exit`    TWO tiles, at x -8, y -3..-2 — a two-tile patch on the
//                      left-hand wall. UNTAGGED. The same three components as
//                      the floor, plus SceneSwitch.cs (guid 4790d855...,
//                      `scene: 1` = Overworld).
//       `No Collision` 85 tiles — MORE tiles than the floor has. TilemapRenderer
//                      only; NO collider of any kind, so a query can never hit it.
//
//   Art: Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/CavesTileSet/ holds 1,829 .asset
//     Tile assets and CaveEntranceTileSet/ holds 250. Each is a MonoBehaviour
//     whose m_Script is Unity's built-in Tile (fileID 13312). `Caves.prefab`
//     and `CavesEntrance.prefab` really are tile palettes: the root carries a
//     `Palette Settings` component (GridPalette, fileID 12395) and a `Layer1`
//     child carrying Tilemap + TilemapRenderer. There is NO Assets/Sprites
//     folder and NO Assets/Tilemaps folder.
//
// PROTECTED BLOCKS — ids are POSITIONAL (src/lib/formify.ts), so nothing here
// is reordered, renumbered, re-counted or re-shaped. The inventory as it stands:
//   socratic  "Socratic: Why a Grid and Not a Pile of Sprites?"  4 <li>, 4 boxes
//             (an item that keeps its '?' keeps its box — rewording one to lose
//             the question mark and end in '.' would drop a box and renumber
//             every box below it in the block)
//   socratic  "Predict Before You Paint"  DELETED WHOLE with the examples block
//             it asked about. Its text is gone from the page, so no saved answer
//             is left sitting under a prompt it does not answer; 7.1:sc1 keeps
//             its four ids, its order and its wording.
// No nested <div> inside any protected block — formify's regex is non-greedy.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';
import type { DecomposeSpec } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The two recurring questions, applied to building a level out of tiles.
// Level 3, so the block withholds what each part DOES and asks the student to
// say it — the parts and their doc pointers are the level-3 bargain. Note that
// a level-3 `does` is never rendered (src/lib/decompose.ts substitutes a gap),
// so nothing below may lean on one as the survivor of an overlap: the rendered
// statements are the part NAMES and the doc pointers.
const DECOMPOSE: DecomposeSpec = {
  big:
    'You are standing in a cave you did not build, and you cannot add a single rock to it. As one ' +
    'problem that is not startable, because "build a level out of tiles" names nothing you could ' +
    'open: there is no paint tray loaded, no known home for the art, and no way to tell which of ' +
    'the grids under the cave is the one that can stop you.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Find the art the cave was cut from, and load a palette',
      does:
        'Two folders under `Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/` hold the cave already sliced into ' +
        'individual **Tile assets** — 1,829 for the cave and 250 for the cave mouth — and the two ' +
        'prefabs beside them, `Caves` and `CavesEntrance`, are palettes with those tiles already ' +
        'arranged. `Window ▸ 2D ▸ Tile Palette` is the tray you paint from, and it opens empty until ' +
        'you point it at one of those two.',
      doc: 'the Project window under `Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/`, the `Window ▸ 2D ▸ Tile Palette` menu entry, and the window tour in 1.2',
      href: '1.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Read the Grid, and treat its cell size as a contract',
      does:
        'The `Grid` component is the snapping rule, and its Cell Size is **1 by 1**. Every distance ' +
        'you have already tuned — the 6.1 jump, the 6.2 check distance, the turret radius — was ' +
        'measured against that unit, so a layout that wants a one-and-a-half-tile gap is a layout ' +
        'that changes, not a grid that changes.',
      doc: 'the `Grid` component in the Inspector for `Platformer ▸ Grid`, and the Inspector reading you practised in 1.2',
      href: '1.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Decide which tilemap you are painting into — solid, exit, or picture',
      does:
        'Three children sit under the Grid. `Collision` is the floor and the walls, `Cave Exit` is a ' +
        'two-tile patch on the left-hand wall that switches scenes, and `No Collision` is backdrop. ' +
        'The first two carry colliders and the third carries none, so which tilemap you select ' +
        'decides whether what you draw can ever stop anything.',
      doc: 'the three children under `Platformer ▸ Grid`, and the tag-versus-layer callout in 6.2 where you put the `Ground` layer on two of them',
      href: '6.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Paint with the five tools the palette gives you',
      does:
        'Paint, Rect, Fill, Erase and the Eye Dropper. An idea becomes a shape by choosing the tile ' +
        'that carries it and then deciding between drawing one cell at a time, filling a rectangle ' +
        'and flooding a region.',
      doc: 'the toolbar of the Tile Palette window, and the build-once-stamp-many habit from 2.4',
      href: '2.4.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Prove the paint is real — stand on it',
      does:
        'Press Play and walk onto what you drew. A tile on `Collision` holds you up because of the ' +
        'collider already sitting on that object; the same tile on `No Collision` is a picture you ' +
        'fall straight through. That one walk is the entire difference between drawing a level and ' +
        'drawing a backdrop.',
      doc: 'the three components on `Platformer ▸ Grid ▸ Collision` in the Inspector, and the ground check and its gizmo from 6.2',
      href: '6.2.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Notice what the split is doing: only one of those five parts is about making a picture. The ' +
    'other four are about which of three grids you are writing into, how far apart the cells are, ' +
    'and whether the thing you drew can stop you. That is the shape of every level-building problem ' +
    'for the rest of the year — the drawing is the easy half, and what a drawing **means** to the ' +
    'physics engine is the half that decides whether you have a level or a wallpaper.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one search? "Build a level out ' +
    'of tiles" cannot be looked up. "Which window holds the tray of tiles" can — and so can "which ' +
    'component decides whether a painted tile can stop me", because that one has an Inspector page ' +
    'with three names on it.',
  turns: [
    'The palette holds finished tiles and the tileset folder holds the art they were cut from. Say which one you drag into the tray, and say what a change to the picture would have to travel through to reach every place you have already painted it.',
    'Your grid\'s cell size is a number somebody chose before you opened the project. Say what would happen to a cave painted at one tile per cell if that number were halved — and say whether the remedy is the grid or the cave.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>7.1 — Tilemaps: The Level Editor</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You are standing in a cave you did not build. The Platformer scene already holds a real
    cave — a floor, walls, and a way out on the left-hand wall — painted onto a grid before
    you ever opened the project. And you cannot add a single rock to it, because you have no
    paint tray and no idea where the art lives. Today you find the art, load the palette, and
    take ownership of a level that is already half-built. There is no script in this lesson:
    the editor is where a level actually lives, and today the editor is the work.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I build a level out of tiles?', 3)}

${renderDecompose('7.1', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/_Scenes</code>, and open
        <code>Platformer.unity</code>. <strong>Game view → press Play</strong> and walk: you can
        move, you can jump, and you stand on rock. Now try to <em>change</em> that rock.
        <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> select things until you find what the floor is made of. You
        will land on a <code>Grid</code> with <strong>three</strong> children under it — and the
        middle one is the reason this hour is about choosing which tilemap you paint into.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Object</th><th>What it is for</th><th>What it already has</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Grid</code></td>
            <td>The snapping contract. Every child tilemap snaps to its cell size, and every system built on top of the level inherits that spacing.</td>
            <td>A <strong>Cell Size of 1 by 1</strong>, and three tilemap children.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Collision</code> (child)</td>
            <td>The rock you stand on. <strong>75 tiles</strong>, filling a box roughly 16 tiles wide and 10 tall — a cave shape, not a solid rectangle.</td>
            <td>A <strong>Tilemap Collider 2D</strong>, a <strong>Composite Collider 2D</strong>, a <strong>Rigidbody 2D</strong> whose Body Type is <strong>Static</strong>, and the tag <code>Ground</code>. Physics already works. Leave all of it alone.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Cave Exit</code> (child)</td>
            <td>The way out. <strong>Two tiles</strong>, at the far left of the floor — the smallest solid thing in the scene, and the one everybody forgets.</td>
            <td>The <em>same three components</em> as the floor, and <strong>Untagged</strong>, because it was built for a different job: <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> sits on it and sends you to the Overworld when you touch it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>No Collision</code> (child)</td>
            <td>Pure picture. Backdrop, decoration, anything the player should see but never bump into. It holds <strong>85 tiles</strong> — more than the floor does.</td>
            <td>A <strong>Tilemap Renderer</strong>, and nothing else. No collider of any kind, so nothing here can ever stop you.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        So the level editor already exists and the physics already works. What is missing is
        everything <em>you</em> need to keep building: the <strong>palette</strong> — the tray of
        tiles you paint from, and the home of the art it is made of.
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/</code>.
            Everything this project owns for the cave is in there. <code>CavesTileSet/</code>
            holds the cave sliced into individual <strong>Tile assets</strong> — <strong>1,829</strong>
            of them, each a small <code>.asset</code> paired with a sprite.
            <code>CaveEntranceTileSet/</code> holds the cave-mouth pieces, <strong>250</strong> of
            them. The two prefabs beside the folders, <code>Caves</code> and
            <code>CavesEntrance</code>, are <strong>Tile Palettes</strong> with those tiles already
            arranged in a grid, and each carries a <code>Palette Settings</code> component that
            says so. The raw source sheets live in
            <code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/OriginalFiles/</code> and
            <code>MainLev2.0.png</code>, for when you need to re-slice. There is no
            <code>Assets/Sprites</code> folder and no <code>Assets/Tilemaps</code> folder in this
            project — if you have been told otherwise, that was for a different project.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Window ▸ 2D ▸ Tile Palette →</strong> the palette selector, and open
            <code>Caves</code>. Tiles fill the window. If you would rather build your own tray,
            create a new palette and drag the <strong>Tile assets</strong> out of
            <code>CavesTileSet/</code> into it — the <code>.asset</code> files, never the raw PNGs,
            because the slicing is already done and re-slicing a finished sheet is a job you take
            on deliberately. Save a palette you make yourself in that same folder, beside the art
            it uses, so it travels with the project.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> select <code>Collision</code> before you paint rock, and
            <code>No Collision</code> before you paint backdrop. Watch the Hierarchy highlight to
            confirm which one has focus: painting into the wrong tilemap is the most common
            first-hour mistake in this unit, and it produces a picture that lies to you.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Tile Palette window →</strong> learn the five tools: Paint (drag to draw), Rect
            (fill a rectangle), Fill (flood a region), Erase, and the Eye Dropper (grab the tile
            already under your cursor). <strong>Scene view →</strong> paint a six-tile-wide ledge
            jutting out of an existing wall, and a two-tile pit floor below it. Then erase half of
            what you drew and redraw it — the point is the hand, not the level.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Scene view →</strong> extend the floor to the right by ten tiles, then
            <strong>Game view → press Play</strong> and walk onto your new floor. If you can stand
            on it, you painted on <code>Collision</code> or <code>Cave Exit</code>. If you fall
            straight through it, you painted on the third grid — and now you know exactly what
            "No Collision" means.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        No file today, so this half holds the two specifications instead — answer them in your
        notes before you touch the palette, because the painting is the easy part and these are
        the decisions it cannot make for you.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the palette and the paint.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>A sprite in this tileset has a <strong>Pixels Per Unit</strong> in its import settings. What is it, and what does it have to agree with for a painted cell to be one world unit?</li>
            <li>If a ledge must sit exactly two tiles above a floor so your <a href="6.1.html">6.1</a> jump clears it, and one cell is one unit, how high is that ledge in world units?</li>
            <li>What is the difference between a Tile asset and the sprite it points at? Which one do you drag into a palette, and which one do you edit when you want to change the picture?</li>
            <li>If you delete a Tile asset that a palette uses, what breaks — the palette, the painted tiles, or both? Predict, then test it on a copy.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: what you are extending.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>When you paint one more tile of rock, which of the three components on <code>Collision</code> notices, and when — immediately, or on the next Play?</li>
            <li>The tilemap also carries a Rigidbody 2D. A floor that never moves is an odd place for a body to live: what is it doing there, and what does the Inspector do to the Gravity Scale field once the body is Static?</li>
            <li><strong>Composite Collider 2D</strong> is load-bearing and you leave it alone today — you meet it properly in <a href="7.2.html">7.2</a>. For now, say what the cave would be like without it, and why "leave the components you inherited alone" is a rule rather than a superstition.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Why a Grid and Not a Pile of Sprites?</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Before today every platform in your level was placed by hand — a sprite dragged in and its Transform typed one number at a time. Which two things did you have to do by hand in that world that the Grid now does for you?</li>
<li>A tilemap stores your level as a grid of numbers plus a table of what each number means. What does that buy you that a scene full of loose sprites cannot offer? Think about file size, and think about how a program could read your level.</li>
<li>The scene already has <em>three</em> tilemaps under one Grid, and two of them are solid while the third is only picture. Why would a designer bother splitting one cave into three grids instead of painting everything into one?</li>
<li>The <code>Collision</code> tilemap carries the tag <code>Ground</code>. Tags belong to GameObjects, not to tiles. What does that tell you about how physics and scripting see a whole tilemap — as many objects, or as one?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Say where the cave art lives, and name the two palette prefabs that ship with it</li>
        <li>Load a palette, and describe the difference between a Tile asset and a sprite</li>
        <li>Name all three tilemaps under the Grid, say which two are solid and which is only picture, and prove it by painting into each</li>
        <li>Name all three components already sitting on <code>Collision</code> and what each is for</li>
        <li>Explain grid-snap as a contract with jumps, colliders and turrets</li>
        <li>Break this task into parts small enough to look each one up on its own, and say in one line what each part does</li>
        <li>Say which earlier lesson each part of this task came from, and where you would reread it</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

